What was looked at
------------------

memcpy   newlib's sh/memcpy.S and memset.S are by Toshiyasu Morita, and
         their code for SH-1 to SH-4 carries no licence. Their SH-5 part
         is SuperH Ltd's.

memcmp   Comes from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
         port_memcmp.c includes. No memcmp in assembly for SuperH exists
         in NetBSD, OpenBSD, newlib, picolibc or klibc. Linux's and
         uClibc's are under the GPL and the LGPL.


What is checked
---------------

clang has no SuperH target, and no build of GNU binutils for it is
published for macOS, so binutils 2.47 is built for sh-elf from its source.
The check runs port_memcpy.S, port_memmove.S and port_memset.S through
clang's preprocessor with none of its own macros, given what GCC 16.1
defines for each processor in its config/sh/sh-c.cc, and assembles them
with that GNU as for SH-2 and SH-2A big-endian, SH-3 and SH-4 little-endian
and SH-4A big-endian, with the _ that sh-elf puts in front of C names, and
for SH-4 with sh-linux's names, which have none. It checks that they define
only the names C reserves and leave nothing undefined. GNU as refuses an
instruction the processor lacks: memcpy.S's bt/s and bf/s fail for SH-1.

port_memcmp.c is not compiled here, as nothing here compiles C for SuperH.
Nothing in this folder has run: before it is relied on, it needs a build
with sh-elf-gcc and a run on hardware or an emulator, such as QEMU's r2d
board, an SH-4.
